From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 16:11:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: G16: let up to three compute commands
 queue behind a running one

With one compute command allowed behind its running predecessor, the
J604 ran compute.light at 124 us per submit (macOS: 21 us). The chain
statistics show why: 30318 commands went ahead with a 32 us gap, but
74866 still ran serially with a ~200 us gap, because every second
command had to wait for the host to notice a retirement and publish
again. macOS keeps many compute commands in the ring at once.

Each command in flight needs its own compute unit, which binds its own
split UMA slot (compute 1 + k of the 32 per class of the 25G83 layout).
There were two. Allocate four: units 2 and 3 take the next 64 MiB
intervals below the render units, so nothing else moves, and only VMs
that run compute allocate them.

g16_horizon becomes a count: the number of compute commands that may
be queued behind a running one, 1..3, default 3. 1 is the previous
behaviour and 0 keeps one compute command at a time.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
